    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nGit is a distributed version control system (DVCS) that tracks changes in source code. Unlike centralized systems (e.g., SVN), every developer has a local copy of the repository history, enabling offline work, faster operations, and better branching/merging.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain Git and how does it differ from other version control systems accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Git is a distributed version control system (DVCS) that tracks changes in source code.\n2. Unlike centralized systems (e.g., SVN), every developer has a local copy of the repository history, enabling offline work, faster operations, and better branching/merging.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA developer can clone a repository, create a local branch, make commits without contacting the server, and later push the work to the remote for collaboration.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: Git is a distributed version control system (DVCS) that tracks changes in source code.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
