    shortDescription: "Clarify the distinction.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nGit is a distributed version control system that runs locally. GitHub is a web-based hosting service for Git repositories that adds collaboration features (pull requests, issues, actions, project management). Git is the tool; GitHub is a platform for hosting and sharing Git repositories.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between git and github and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Git is a distributed version control system that runs locally.\n2. GitHub is a web-based hosting service for Git repositories that adds collaboration features (pull requests, issues, actions, project management).\n3. Git is the tool; GitHub is a platform for hosting and sharing Git repositories.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nA developer can clone a repository, create a local branch, make commits without contacting the server, and later push the work to the remote for collaboration.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: Git is a distributed version control system that runs locally.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
