    shortDescription: "Explain the command.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse `git branch <branch-name>` to create a new branch. To switch to it, use `git checkout <branch-name>` or `git switch <branch-name>`. Alternatively, `git checkout -b <branch-name>` creates and switches to the new branch in one command.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you create a new branch, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use `git branch <branch-name>` to create a new branch.\n2. To switch to it, use `git checkout <branch-name>` or `git switch <branch-name>`.\n3. Alternatively, `git checkout -b <branch-name>` creates and switches to the new branch in one command.\n4. Relate the operation to branch history and state, then distinguish local changes from changes already shared with other developers..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a shared codebase can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Use `git branch <branch-name>` to create a new branch.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: Use `git branch <branch-name>` to create a new branch.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
