    question: "What is the `HEAD` in Git?",
    slug: "git-head",
    shortDescription: "Explain the pointer.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`HEAD` is a pointer to the current branch reference (or commit if you're in detached HEAD state). It indicates which branch is currently checked out. When you make a new commit, `HEAD` moves to the new commit.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `HEAD` in Git accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `HEAD` is a pointer to the current branch reference (or commit if you're in detached HEAD state).\n2. It indicates which branch is currently checked out.\n3. When you make a new commit, `HEAD` moves to the new commit.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter checking out `feature`, `HEAD` refers to that branch; after a new commit, the branch and `HEAD` both move to the new commit.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `HEAD` is a pointer to the current branch reference (or commit if you're in detached HEAD state).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
