    slug: "git-add",
    shortDescription: "Explain staging.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git add <file>` stages changes (adds them to the index) to be included in the next commit. It tells Git you want to include the changes in the snapshot. You can add specific files or all changes with `git add.`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain What does `git add` do accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git add <file>` stages changes (adds them to the index) to be included in the next commit.\n2. It tells Git you want to include the changes in the snapshot.\n3. You can add specific files or all changes with `git add.`.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nEdit `src/auth.ts`, run `git add src/auth.ts`, then `git status`; the file should appear under staged changes, while later edits remain unstaged until added again.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git add <file>` stages changes (adds them to the index) to be included in the next commit.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
