    slug: "git-commit",
    shortDescription: "Explain the commit operation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git commit -m \"message\"` creates a new commit by taking the staged changes and saving them as a snapshot in the repository. The commit message should describe the changes concisely. It advances the branch pointer.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain What does `git commit` do accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git commit -m \"message\"` creates a new commit by taking the staged changes and saving them as a snapshot in the repository.\n2. The commit message should describe the changes concisely.\n3. It advances the branch pointer.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nStage the intended changes and run `git commit -m \"Validate login input\"`; the staged snapshot becomes a new commit, while unrelated working-tree changes are not included.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git commit -m \"message\"` creates a new commit by taking the staged changes and saving them as a snapshot in the repository.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
