    question: "What is the difference between `git log` and `git reflog`?",
    slug: "log-vs-reflog",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git log` shows the commit history reachable from the current branch. `git reflog` shows a local record of when branch tips and other references were updated, allowing you to recover lost commits (e.g., after a reset or rebase). Reflog is local and not shared.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `git log` and `git reflog` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git log` shows the commit history reachable from the current branch.\n2. `git reflog` shows a local record of when branch tips and other references were updated, allowing you to recover lost commits (e.g., after a reset or rebase).\n3. Reflog is local and not shared.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter an accidental reset, run `git reflog`, identify the previous branch position, and inspect it before recovering the work.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git log` shows the commit history reachable from the current branch.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
