    focus: null,
    question: "What is the difference between `git reset` and `git revert`?",
    slug: "reset-vs-revert",
    shortDescription: "Explain the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git reset` moves the branch pointer and can change history (dangerous on shared branches). It has modes: `--soft`, `--mixed`, `--hard`. `git revert` creates a new commit that undoes the changes of a previous commit, preserving history and safe for public branches.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `git reset` and `git revert` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git reset` moves the branch pointer and can change history (dangerous on shared branches).\n2. It has modes: `--soft`, `--mixed`, `--hard`.\n3. `git revert` creates a new commit that undoes the changes of a previous commit, preserving history and safe for public branches.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nOn a private branch, use `git reset --soft HEAD~1` to move the branch back while keeping the changes staged; use `--hard` only when you intentionally want to discard tracked working-tree changes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git reset` moves the branch pointer and can change history (dangerous on shared branches).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
