    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git clone <url>` creates a local copy of a remote repository. By default it fetches repository history into a new local repository, configures the remote-tracking setup, and checks out an initial branch. Shallow and partial-clone options can fetch less data. It's the standard way to start working on an existing project.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you clone a repository, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git clone <url>` creates a local copy of a remote repository.\n2. By default it fetches repository history into a new local repository, configures the remote-tracking setup, and checks out an initial branch.\n3. Shallow and partial-clone options can fetch less data.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a shared codebase can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. `git clone <url>` creates a local copy of a remote repository.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git clone <url>` creates a local copy of a remote repository.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
