    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git clone` copies a repository from a remote to your local machine. A fork is a copy of a repository on GitHub (or other platforms) under your own account, allowing you to make changes without affecting the original repository. Forking is a server-side operation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `git clone` and `git fork` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git clone` copies a repository from a remote to your local machine.\n2. A fork is a copy of a repository on GitHub (or other platforms) under your own account, allowing you to make changes without affecting the original repository.\n3. Forking is a server-side operation.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `git clone` copies a repository from a remote to your local machine.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git clone` copies a repository from a remote to your local machine.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
