    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git remote add <name> <url>` adds a new remote. For example, `git remote add origin https://github.com/user/repo.git`. Then you can push/pull to that remote.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you add a remote repository, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git remote add <name> <url>` adds a new remote.\n2. For example, `git remote add origin https://github.com/user/repo.git`.\n3. Then you can push/pull to that remote.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a shared codebase can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. `git remote add <name> <url>` adds a new remote.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git remote add <name> <url>` adds a new remote.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
