    slug: "git-push",
    shortDescription: "Explain pushing changes.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git push <remote> <branch>` uploads local commits to the remote repository. It updates the remote branch to match your local branch. For example, `git push origin main` pushes the main branch to the origin remote.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain What does `git push` do accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git push <remote> <branch>` uploads local commits to the remote repository.\n2. It updates the remote branch to match your local branch.\n3. For example, `git push origin main` pushes the main branch to the origin remote.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `git push <remote> <branch>` uploads local commits to the remote repository.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git push <remote> <branch>` uploads local commits to the remote repository.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
