    slug: "git-pull",
    shortDescription: "Explain pulling changes.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git pull <remote> <branch>` fetches changes from the remote repository and merges them into your local branch. It is a combination of `git fetch` and `git merge`. Use `git pull --rebase` to rebase instead of merge.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain What does `git pull` do accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git pull <remote> <branch>` fetches changes from the remote repository and merges them into your local branch.\n2. It is a combination of `git fetch` and `git merge`.\n3. Use `git pull --rebase` to rebase instead of merge.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nWith local work already committed or stashed, run `git pull --rebase origin main` when that workflow matches the project policy, then resolve any conflicts before continuing.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git pull <remote> <branch>` fetches changes from the remote repository and merges them into your local branch.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
