    question: "What is the difference between `git pull` and `git fetch`?",
    slug: "pull-vs-fetch",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git fetch` downloads new commits from the remote but does not integrate them into your local branch. `git pull` downloads and then merges (or rebases) the changes into your current branch. Fetch is safer as it allows you to review changes before merging.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `git pull` and `git fetch` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git fetch` downloads new commits from the remote but does not integrate them into your local branch.\n2. `git pull` downloads and then merges (or rebases) the changes into your current branch.\n3. Fetch is safer as it allows you to review changes before merging.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nRun `git fetch origin` and inspect `origin/main` before changing your local branch; you can compare the incoming commits without merging them.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git fetch` downloads new commits from the remote but does not integrate them into your local branch.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git fetch` downloads new commits from the remote but does not integrate them into your local branch. `git pull` downloads and then merges (or rebases) the changes into your current branch. Fetch is safer as it allows you to review changes before merging.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `git pull` and `git fetch` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git fetch` downloads new commits from the remote but does not integrate them into your local branch.\n2. `git pull` downloads and then merges (or rebases) the changes into your current branch.\n3. Fetch is safer as it allows you to review changes before merging.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nRun `git fetch origin` and inspect `origin/main` before changing your local branch; you can compare the incoming commits without merging them.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git fetch` downloads new commits from the remote but does not integrate them into your local branch.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
