    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nGitHub Flow is a lightweight branching model where `main` is always deployable. Feature branches are created from `main`, merged back via pull requests after review, and then deployed. It's simple and works well for continuous delivery.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain GitHub Flow accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. GitHub Flow is a lightweight branching model where `main` is always deployable.\n2. Feature branches are created from `main`, merged back via pull requests after review, and then deployed.\n3. It's simple and works well for continuous delivery.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. GitHub Flow is a lightweight branching model where `main` is always deployable.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: GitHub Flow is a lightweight branching model where `main` is always deployable.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
