    shortDescription: "List advantages and disadvantages.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPros: Clean, linear history; easier to understand; avoids merge commits. Cons: Rewrites history, can cause conflicts for others; dangerous if used on shared branches; can be complex for beginners.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks engineering judgment around what are the pros and cons of rebasing. A useful answer makes the benefit, cost, and condition for choosing the approach explicit.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Pros: Clean, linear history; easier to understand; avoids merge commits.\n2. Cons: Rewrites history, can cause conflicts for others; dangerous if used on shared branches; can be complex for beginners.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what you gain, what you give up, and what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Pros: Clean, linear history; easier to understand; avoids merge commits.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: Pros: Clean, linear history; easier to understand; avoids merge commits.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Listing benefits and drawbacks without saying when they matter.\n- Treating one approach as universally superior.\n- Ignoring operational cost, maintenance, or failure behavior.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nName the constraint that makes the trade-off acceptable, then state when you would deliberately choose another approach.",
