    question: "What is an interactive rebase?",
    slug: "interactive-rebase",
    shortDescription: "Explain the feature.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git rebase -i <commit>` allows you to modify the commit history interactively. You can reorder, squash (combine), edit, drop, or reword commits. It's useful for cleaning up local commits before merging.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain an interactive rebase accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git rebase -i <commit>` allows you to modify the commit history interactively.\n2. You can reorder, squash (combine), edit, drop, or reword commits.\n3. It's useful for cleaning up local commits before merging.\n4. Relate the operation to branch history and state, then distinguish local changes from changes already shared with other developers..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `git rebase -i <commit>` allows you to modify the commit history interactively.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git rebase -i <commit>` allows you to modify the commit history interactively.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating a rebase as a harmless rewrite of shared history.\n- Forgetting that rewritten commits get different object IDs.\n- Force-pushing without checking whether collaborators depend on the remote branch.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
