    slug: "detached-head",
    shortDescription: "Explain the situation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDetached HEAD occurs when HEAD points directly to a commit instead of a branch. You are not on any branch. Any commits you make will be orphaned unless you create a new branch to reference them. It is common when checking out a specific commit or tag.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a detached HEAD state accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Detached HEAD occurs when HEAD points directly to a commit instead of a branch.\n2. You are not on any branch.\n3. Any commits you make will be orphaned unless you create a new branch to reference them.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter checking out `feature`, `HEAD` refers to that branch; after a new commit, the branch and `HEAD` both move to the new commit.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: Detached HEAD occurs when HEAD points directly to a commit instead of a branch.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
