    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git stash` temporarily saves local changes (working directory and staged) without committing them, allowing you to pull new changes or switch branches. Use when you need to clean your working directory quickly. Apply them back with `git stash pop` or `git stash apply`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain `git stash` and when would you use it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git stash` temporarily saves local changes (working directory and staged) without committing them, allowing you to pull new changes or switch branches.\n2. Use when you need to clean your working directory quickly.\n3. Apply them back with `git stash pop` or `git stash apply`.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nUse `git stash push -u` to temporarily store local changes when you need a clean working tree for another task; re-apply only after checking for conflicts.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git stash` temporarily saves local changes (working directory and staged) without committing them, allowing you to pull new changes or switch branches.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
