    slug: "stash-pop-vs-apply",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git stash apply` applies the stashed changes but keeps the stash in the list. `git stash pop` applies and then drops the stash from the list. Use `pop` if you no longer need the stash.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `git stash pop` and `git stash apply` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git stash apply` applies the stashed changes but keeps the stash in the list.\n2. `git stash pop` applies and then drops the stash from the list.\n3. Use `pop` if you no longer need the stash.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nUse `git stash push -u` to temporarily store local changes when you need a clean working tree for another task; re-apply only after checking for conflicts.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git stash apply` applies the stashed changes but keeps the stash in the list.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
