    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git cherry-pick <commit-hash>` applies the changes from a specific commit to your current branch. It is useful for selectively applying patches, backporting fixes, or moving specific commits without merging entire branches.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain `git cherry-pick` accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git cherry-pick <commit-hash>` applies the changes from a specific commit to your current branch.\n2. It is useful for selectively applying patches, backporting fixes, or moving specific commits without merging entire branches.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nOn a release branch, `git cherry-pick <commit>` can bring one specific fix across from another branch when that isolated change is appropriate.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git cherry-pick <commit-hash>` applies the changes from a specific commit to your current branch.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
