    shortDescription: "Explain the binary search debugging tool.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git bisect` helps find the commit that introduced a bug by performing a binary search over the commit history. You mark a commit as 'good' (working) and a commit as 'bad' (broken), and Git repeatedly checks out commits for you to test.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain `git bisect` and how do you use it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git bisect` helps find the commit that introduced a bug by performing a binary search over the commit history.\n2. You mark a commit as 'good' (working) and a commit as 'bad' (broken), and Git repeatedly checks out commits for you to test.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a regression starts somewhere between a known-good and known-bad commit, `git bisect` can repeatedly test a midpoint until the first bad commit is identified.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git bisect` helps find the commit that introduced a bug by performing a binary search over the commit history.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
