    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git blame <file>` annotates a file line by line, showing the commit hash, author, and timestamp for each line. It is used to determine who last modified a specific line, useful for understanding code changes and accountability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain `git blame` accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git blame <file>` annotates a file line by line, showing the commit hash, author, and timestamp for each line.\n2. It is used to determine who last modified a specific line, useful for understanding code changes and accountability.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nRun `git blame src/payment/PaymentService.java` to identify which commit last changed each line, then inspect the commit for context rather than treating blame as proof of fault.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git blame <file>` annotates a file line by line, showing the commit hash, author, and timestamp for each line.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
