    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA draft pull request is a PR that is not ready for review (marked as 'draft'). It cannot be merged until converted to a regular PR. It's useful for early collaboration or to signal work in progress.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a draft pull request and a regular pull request and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A draft pull request is a PR that is not ready for review (marked as 'draft').\n2. It cannot be merged until converted to a regular PR.\n3. It's useful for early collaboration or to signal work in progress.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A draft pull request is a PR that is not ready for review (marked as 'draft').\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: A draft pull request is a PR that is not ready for review (marked as 'draft').\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
