    slug: "force-vs-force-with-lease",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`--force` overwrites the remote branch regardless of its state. `--force-with-lease` checks if the remote branch has been updated since you last fetched; if it has, the push fails, preventing you from accidentally overwriting someone else's commits.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `git push --force` and `git push --force-with-lease` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `--force` overwrites the remote branch regardless of its state.\n2. `--force-with-lease` checks if the remote branch has been updated since you last fetched; if it has, the push fails, preventing you from accidentally overwriting someone else's commits.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `--force` overwrites the remote branch regardless of its state.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `--force` overwrites the remote branch regardless of its state.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
