    shortDescription: "Explain the clone type.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA shallow clone (`git clone --depth 1`) downloads only the latest commit history. It reduces clone time and disk space. Use it for CI/CD pipelines or when you only need the latest version, not full history.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a shallow clone and when would you use it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A shallow clone (`git clone --depth 1`) downloads only the latest commit history.\n2. It reduces clone time and disk space.\n3. Use it for CI/CD pipelines or when you only need the latest version, not full history.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A shallow clone (`git clone --depth 1`) downloads only the latest commit history.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: A shallow clone (`git clone --depth 1`) downloads only the latest commit history.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
