    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git bundle` creates a file that contains Git objects (commits, references) and can be used to transfer the repository offline. `git archive` creates a tar or zip of the files at a specific commit, without Git metadata.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `git bundle` and `git archive` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git bundle` creates a file that contains Git objects (commits, references) and can be used to transfer the repository offline.\n2. `git archive` creates a tar or zip of the files at a specific commit, without Git metadata.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `git bundle` creates a file that contains Git objects (commits, references) and can be used to transfer the repository offline.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git bundle` creates a file that contains Git objects (commits, references) and can be used to transfer the repository offline.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
