    question: "What is the difference between `git rebase --onto` and regular rebase?",
    slug: "rebase-onto",
    shortDescription: "Explain the advanced option.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`git rebase --onto <newbase> <upstream> <branch>` moves commits from `<upstream>` to `<branch>` onto `<newbase>` instead of the base of the current branch. It allows you to rebase onto a different branch, useful for re-parenting.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `git rebase --onto` and regular rebase and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `git rebase --onto <newbase> <upstream> <branch>` moves commits from `<upstream>` to `<branch>` onto `<newbase>` instead of the base of the current branch.\n2. It allows you to rebase onto a different branch, useful for re-parenting.\n3. Relate the operation to branch history and state, then distinguish local changes from changes already shared with other developers..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `git rebase --onto <newbase> <upstream> <branch>` moves commits from `<upstream>` to `<branch>` onto `<newbase>` instead of the base of the current branch.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: `git rebase --onto <newbase> <upstream> <branch>` moves commits from `<upstream>` to `<branch>` onto `<newbase>` instead of the base of the current branch.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
