    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nGit uses SHA-1 (now transitioning to SHA-256) to generate a 40-character checksum for each object (blob, tree, commit). This hash serves as a unique identifier for content. It ensures integrity; any change to content changes the hash.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a SHA-1 hash and how is it used in Git accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Git uses SHA-1 (now transitioning to SHA-256) to generate a 40-character checksum for each object (blob, tree, commit).\n2. This hash serves as a unique identifier for content.\n3. It ensures integrity; any change to content changes the hash.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Git uses SHA-1 (now transitioning to SHA-256) to generate a 40-character checksum for each object (blob, tree, commit).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: Git uses SHA-1 (now transitioning to SHA-256) to generate a 40-character checksum for each object (blob, tree, commit).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
