    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe index is a binary file (`.git/index`) that holds a snapshot of the files as they will be in the next commit. It acts as the staging area between the working directory and the repository. Commands like `git add` update the index.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the index (staging area) in Git accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The index is a binary file (`.git/index`) that holds a snapshot of the files as they will be in the next commit.\n2. It acts as the staging area between the working directory and the repository.\n3. Commands like `git add` update the index.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. The index is a binary file (`.git/index`) that holds a snapshot of the files as they will be in the next commit.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: The index is a binary file (`.git/index`) that holds a snapshot of the files as they will be in the next commit.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
