    question: "What is the difference between a fast-forward and a recursive merge at the object level?",
    slug: "merge-internals",
    shortDescription: "Explain the internal difference.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFast-forward: Git moves the branch pointer to the new commit without creating a merge commit. A non-fast-forward merge uses a three-way merge; for an ordinary two-head merge, current Git uses the `ort` strategy by default: Git creates a new merge commit with two parents, and the tree of that commit is the result of merging the two branches.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a fast-forward and a recursive merge at the object level and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Fast-forward: Git moves the branch pointer to the new commit without creating a merge commit.\n2. A non-fast-forward merge uses a three-way merge; for an ordinary two-head merge, current Git uses the `ort` strategy by default: Git creates a new merge commit with two parents, and the tree of that commit is the result of merging the two branches.\n3. Relate the operation to branch history and state, then distinguish local changes from changes already shared with other developers..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Fast-forward: Git moves the branch pointer to the new commit without creating a merge commit.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: Fast-forward: Git moves the branch pointer to the new commit without creating a merge commit.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFast-forward: Git moves the branch pointer to the new commit without creating a merge commit. A non-fast-forward merge uses a three-way merge; for an ordinary two-head merge, current Git uses the `ort` strategy by default: Git creates a new merge commit with two parents, and the tree of that commit is the result of merging the two branches.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a fast-forward and a recursive merge at the object level and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Fast-forward: Git moves the branch pointer to the new commit without creating a merge commit.\n2. A non-fast-forward merge uses a three-way merge; for an ordinary two-head merge, current Git uses the `ort` strategy by default: Git creates a new merge commit with two parents, and the tree of that commit is the result of merging the two branches.\n3. Relate the operation to branch history and state, then distinguish local changes from changes already shared with other developers..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Fast-forward: Git moves the branch pointer to the new commit without creating a merge commit.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: Fast-forward: Git moves the branch pointer to the new commit without creating a merge commit.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
