    shortDescription: "Explain the reflog storage.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe reflog is stored in `.git/logs/` as plain text files. It records when branch tips and HEAD are updated. Each entry contains the old and new hashes, the committer, and a timestamp. It is local to the repository.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the reflog and how is it stored internally accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The reflog is stored in `.git/logs/` as plain text files.\n2. It records when branch tips and HEAD are updated.\n3. Each entry contains the old and new hashes, the committer, and a timestamp.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter an accidental reset, run `git reflog`, identify the previous branch position, and inspect it before recovering the work.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: The reflog is stored in `.git/logs/` as plain text files.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nAnswer with the repository state in mind. Git questions become clearer when you explain which reference, index entry, working-tree file, or commit changes.",
