    question: "What is the difference between a local branch and a remote-tracking branch?",
    slug: "local-vs-remote-tracking",
    shortDescription: "Explain the distinction.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA local branch is a branch you work on locally. A remote-tracking branch (e.g., `origin/main`) is a read-only local copy of a branch from a remote, updated on `git fetch` or `git pull`. It tracks the state of the remote branch.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a local branch and a remote-tracking branch and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A local branch is a branch you work on locally.\n2. A remote-tracking branch (e.g., `origin/main`) is a read-only local copy of a branch from a remote, updated on `git fetch` or `git pull`.\n3. It tracks the state of the remote branch.\n4. Relate the operation to branch history and state, then distinguish local changes from changes already shared with other developers..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a shared codebase, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A local branch is a branch you work on locally.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a shared codebase, this affects how engineers create, review, integrate, or recover changes. The relevant Git behavior here is: A local branch is a branch you work on locally.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
