    question: "What is a structured interview and why is it more effective than unstructured?",
    slug: "structured-interview",
    shortDescription: "Explain the format and benefits.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA structured interview uses a standardized set of questions asked in the same order to all candidates, with a pre-defined scoring system. It reduces bias, improves reliability, and allows for objective comparison. Unstructured interviews are more conversational but prone to inconsistency and subjective judgments. The important point is that structure should be paired with job-relevant competencies and consistent evaluation criteria, while still allowing relevant probing for clarification.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you understand what is a structured interview and why is it more effective than unstructured well enough to apply it consistently in a real people process. A strong answer balances practical judgment with policy and employee impact.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A structured interview uses a standardized set of questions asked in the same order to all candidates, with a pre-defined scoring system.\n2. It reduces bias, improves reliability, and allows for objective comparison.\n3. Unstructured interviews are more conversational but prone to inconsistency and subjective judgments.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an HR process, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A structured interview uses a standardized set of questions asked in the same order to all candidates, with a pre-defined scoring system.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Applying a process without considering the actual role, customer, policy, or business context.\n- Making a decision without documenting the relevant evidence or assumptions.\n- Using an absolute rule where the facts of the case should drive the decision.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
