    slug: "headhunting-vs-recruiting",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHeadhunting is a proactive approach targeting specific individuals (often passive candidates) for senior or specialist roles. It involves direct outreach, networking, and often uses external agencies. Traditional recruiting is broader, advertising open positions and screening applicants. Headhunting is more personalized and expensive.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis is asked to see whether you can separate two HR practices and explain the employment or process consequence of the difference. Strong answers also show when each approach is appropriate.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Headhunting is a proactive approach targeting specific individuals (often passive candidates) for senior or specialist roles.\n2. It involves direct outreach, networking, and often uses external agencies.\n3. Traditional recruiting is broader, advertising open positions and screening applicants.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an HR process, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Headhunting is a proactive approach targeting specific individuals (often passive candidates) for senior or specialist roles.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
