    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n1) Listen to both parties individually to understand their perspectives. 2) Gather facts and documentation. 3) Arrange a mediation meeting with both parties (and a neutral HR representative). 4) Encourage open dialogue and find common ground. 5) Propose solutions and agree on a plan. 6) Follow up to ensure resolution. Maintain confidentiality and neutrality throughout.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer wants to hear a workable HR process, not just a definition of how would you handle an employee relations issue (e.g., conflict between two employees). Include the decision points, documentation, and fairness or compliance considerations that matter.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. 1) Listen to both parties individually to understand their perspectives.\n2. 2) Gather facts and documentation.\n3. 3) Arrange a mediation meeting with both parties (and a neutral HR representative).\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in an HR process can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. 1) Listen to both parties individually to understand their perspectives.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
