    shortDescription: "Explain strategies to reduce absenteeism.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHR can: 1) Implement clear attendance policies, 2) Track patterns (using HRIS), 3) Conduct return-to-work interviews, 4) Offer wellness programs to reduce sick leave, 5) Promote flexible working, 6) Address underlying issues (morale, engagement), and 7) Provide support for personal problems (EAP).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you understand what is the role of hr in managing absenteeism well enough to apply it consistently in a real people process. A strong answer balances practical judgment with policy and employee impact.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. HR can: 1) Implement clear attendance policies, 2) Track patterns (using HRIS), 3) Conduct return-to-work interviews, 4) Offer wellness programs to reduce sick leave, 5) Promote flexible working, 6) Address underlying issues (morale, engagement), and 7) Provide support for personal problems (EAP).\n2. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an HR process, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. HR can: 1) Implement clear attendance policies, 2) Track patterns (using HRIS), 3) Conduct return-to-work interviews, 4) Offer wellness programs to reduce sick leave, 5) Promote flexible working, 6) Address underlying issues (morale, engagement), and 7) Provide support for personal problems (EAP).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Applying a process without considering the actual role, customer, policy, or business context.\n- Making a decision without documenting the relevant evidence or assumptions.\n- Using an absolute rule where the facts of the case should drive the decision.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
