    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n1) Encourage the employee to use the EAP. 2) Offer flexible work arrangements. 3) Provide information on mental health resources. 4) Have a supportive conversation about accommodations. 5) Respect confidentiality and privacy. 6) Normalize conversations about mental health to reduce stigma. 7) Train managers to recognize signs and refer to HR.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer wants to hear a workable HR process, not just a definition of how do you handle an employee who is experiencing mental health issues. Include the decision points, documentation, and fairness or compliance considerations that matter.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. 1) Encourage the employee to use the EAP.\n2. 2) Offer flexible work arrangements.\n3. 3) Provide information on mental health resources.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the operation in the order you would actually perform it: prerequisite, main action, verification, and the important failure case.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in an HR process can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. 1) Encourage the employee to use the EAP.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
