    slug: "retention-strategy",
    shortDescription: "List key elements.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nComponents: 1) Competitive compensation and benefits, 2) Career development and growth opportunities, 3) Recognition and rewards, 4) Positive work culture and engagement, 5) Work-life balance, 6) Effective leadership and management, 7) Regular feedback and communication, 8) Diversity and inclusion, 9) Employee wellness programs, 10) Exit interview analysis to identify issues.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you understand what are the key components of an employee retention strategy well enough to apply it consistently in a real people process. A strong answer balances practical judgment with policy and employee impact.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Components: 1) Competitive compensation and benefits, 2) Career development and growth opportunities, 3) Recognition and rewards, 4) Positive work culture and engagement, 5) Work-life balance, 6) Effective leadership and management, 7) Regular feedback and communication, 8) Diversity and inclusion, 9) Employee wellness programs, 10) Exit interview analysis to identify issues.\n2. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the requested list directly, then go deeper on the item most relevant to the interviewer’s context.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an HR process, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Components: 1) Competitive compensation and benefits, 2) Career development and growth opportunities, 3) Recognition and rewards, 4) Positive work culture and engagement, 5) Work-life balance, 6) Effective leadership and management, 7) Regular feedback and communication, 8) Diversity and inclusion, 9) Employee wellness programs, 10) Exit interview analysis to identify issues.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
