    slug: "pm-vs-appraisal",
    shortDescription: "Clarify the concepts.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPerformance management is a continuous, strategic process focused on development and improvement. Performance appraisal is a specific event (usually annual) where performance is formally evaluated. Performance management encompasses appraisals but also includes ongoing coaching, feedback, and goal-setting. Any AI-assisted process should be validated for job relevance, monitored for disparate impact or other bias, and kept subject to appropriate human oversight. The strongest approach is continuous rather than relying only on an annual review: goals, regular feedback, development, and documented follow-through should connect.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis is asked to see whether you can separate two HR practices and explain the employment or process consequence of the difference. Strong answers also show when each approach is appropriate.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Performance management is a continuous, strategic process focused on development and improvement.\n2. Performance appraisal is a specific event (usually annual) where performance is formally evaluated.\n3. Performance management encompasses appraisals but also includes ongoing coaching, feedback, and goal-setting.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an HR process, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Performance management is a continuous, strategic process focused on development and improvement.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
