    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n1) Prepare in advance (review goals, gather feedback). 2) Schedule a private meeting with the employee. 3) Create a comfortable, two-way dialogue. 4) Provide specific, constructive feedback backed by examples. 5) Acknowledge achievements. 6) Discuss areas for improvement and development. 7) Set new goals for the next period. 8) Document the discussion and agreed actions. Any AI-assisted process should be validated for job relevance, monitored for disparate impact or other bias, and kept subject to appropriate human oversight.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer wants to hear a workable HR process, not just a definition of how do you conduct a performance appraisal effectively. Include the decision points, documentation, and fairness or compliance considerations that matter.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. 1) Prepare in advance (review goals, gather feedback).\n2. 2) Schedule a private meeting with the employee.\n3. 3) Create a comfortable, two-way dialogue.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in an HR process can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. 1) Prepare in advance (review goals, gather feedback).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
