    slug: "fair-compensation",
    shortDescription: "List the principles.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPrinciples: Internal equity (fairness within the organization), External competitiveness (market alignment), Transparency (clear communication), Legal compliance (equal pay laws), Performance orientation (rewarding merit), and Affordability (sustainable for the business). Any AI-assisted process should be validated for job relevance, monitored for disparate impact or other bias, and kept subject to appropriate human oversight.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you understand what are the key principles of a fair compensation system well enough to apply it consistently in a real people process. A strong answer balances practical judgment with policy and employee impact.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Principles: Internal equity (fairness within the organization), External competitiveness (market alignment), Transparency (clear communication), Legal compliance (equal pay laws), Performance orientation (rewarding merit), and Affordability (sustainable for the business).\n2. Any AI-assisted process should be validated for job relevance, monitored for disparate impact or other bias, and kept subject to appropriate human oversight.\n3. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an HR process, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Principles: Internal equity (fairness within the organization), External competitiveness (market alignment), Transparency (clear communication), Legal compliance (equal pay laws), Performance orientation (rewarding merit), and Affordability (sustainable for the business).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
