    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTraining focuses on specific, job-related skills needed for current roles (e.g., software training). Development is broader, focusing on long-term growth (leadership, strategic thinking). Training is often short-term, development is long-term and continuous. Any AI-assisted process should be validated for job relevance, monitored for disparate impact or other bias, and kept subject to appropriate human oversight.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis is asked to see whether you can separate two HR practices and explain the employment or process consequence of the difference. Strong answers also show when each approach is appropriate.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Training focuses on specific, job-related skills needed for current roles (e.g., software training).\n2. Development is broader, focusing on long-term growth (leadership, strategic thinking).\n3. Training is often short-term, development is long-term and continuous.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an HR process, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Training focuses on specific, job-related skills needed for current roles (e.g., software training).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
