    shortDescription: "Explain the process.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n1) Identify key stakeholders and their needs. 2) Define KPIs aligned with business goals. 3) Gather data from HRIS, surveys, and other sources. 4) Choose visualization tools (e.g., Power BI, Tableau). 5) Design a user-friendly dashboard with drill-down capabilities. 6) Regularly update and review with stakeholders.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer wants to hear a workable HR process, not just a definition of how do you develop an hr dashboard. Include the decision points, documentation, and fairness or compliance considerations that matter.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. 1) Identify key stakeholders and their needs.\n2. 2) Define KPIs aligned with business goals.\n3. 3) Gather data from HRIS, surveys, and other sources.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in an HR process can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. 1) Identify key stakeholders and their needs.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
