    shortDescription: "Explain compliance and best practices.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nEmployee data privacy is critical for trust and legal compliance (GDPR, CCPA). HR must: 1) Only collect necessary data, 2) Store data securely (encryption, access controls), 3) Limit access to authorized personnel, 4) Provide transparency (privacy policies), 5) Obtain consent where required, 6) Respond to data subject requests, and 7) Regularly audit data practices. The exact legal requirements depend on jurisdiction and context, so HR should apply the organization's privacy policy and involve legal or privacy specialists when required.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you understand what is the importance of employee data privacy and how do you ensure it well enough to apply it consistently in a real people process. A strong answer balances practical judgment with policy and employee impact.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Employee data privacy is critical for trust and legal compliance (GDPR, CCPA).\n2. HR must: 1) Only collect necessary data, 2) Store data securely (encryption, access controls), 3) Limit access to authorized personnel, 4) Provide transparency (privacy policies), 5) Obtain consent where required, 6) Respond to data subject requests, and 7) Regularly audit data practices.\n3. The exact legal requirements depend on jurisdiction and context, so HR should apply the organization's privacy policy and involve legal or privacy specialists when required.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an HR process, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Employee data privacy is critical for trust and legal compliance (GDPR, CCPA).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Applying a process without considering the actual role, customer, policy, or business context.\n- Making a decision without documenting the relevant evidence or assumptions.\n- Using an absolute rule where the facts of the case should drive the decision.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
