    slug: "harassment-complaint-handling",
    shortDescription: "Explain the process.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n1) Take the complaint seriously. 2) Ensure confidentiality to the extent possible. 3) Conduct a prompt and thorough investigation by a trained investigator. 4) Interview all relevant parties. 5) Gather documentation. 6) Reach a conclusion and take appropriate action (counseling, training, discipline, up to termination). 7) Communicate the outcome to the complainant (without revealing confidential details). 8) Follow up to prevent retaliation. Any AI-assisted process should be validated for job relevance, monitored for disparate impact or other bias, and kept subject to appropriate human oversight. The process should protect against retaliation and preserve confidentiality to the extent practical, while avoiding promises of absolute confidentiality.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer wants to hear a workable HR process, not just a definition of how do you handle a workplace harassment complaint. Include the decision points, documentation, and fairness or compliance considerations that matter.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. 1) Take the complaint seriously.\n2. 2) Ensure confidentiality to the extent possible.\n3. 3) Conduct a prompt and thorough investigation by a trained investigator.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the operation in the order you would actually perform it: prerequisite, main action, verification, and the important failure case.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in an HR process can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. 1) Take the complaint seriously.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
