    slug: "contractor-vs-employee",
    shortDescription: "Explain the classification.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn employee is controlled by the employer (how, when, and where work is done) and receives benefits. An independent contractor operates independently, controls their own work, and usually provides their own tools. Misclassification can result in significant penalties (taxes, benefits). HR must use the IRS guidelines to classify correctly.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis is asked to see whether you can separate two HR practices and explain the employment or process consequence of the difference. Strong answers also show when each approach is appropriate.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. An employee is controlled by the employer (how, when, and where work is done) and receives benefits.\n2. An independent contractor operates independently, controls their own work, and usually provides their own tools.\n3. Misclassification can result in significant penalties (taxes, benefits).\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an HR process, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. An employee is controlled by the employer (how, when, and where work is done) and receives benefits.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
