    shortDescription: "Explain the steps.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n1) Collect and verify employee data (hours, overtime). 2) Calculate gross pay, deductions (taxes, benefits). 3) Process payroll using HRIS/payroll system. 4) Review for accuracy. 5) Obtain approval. 6) Distribute pay (direct deposit or checks). 7) Provide pay stubs. 8) File tax reports. 9) Maintain records. Ensure compliance with federal and state tax laws.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer wants to hear a workable HR process, not just a definition of how do you handle payroll processing accurately. Include the decision points, documentation, and fairness or compliance considerations that matter.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. 1) Collect and verify employee data (hours, overtime).\n2. 2) Calculate gross pay, deductions (taxes, benefits).\n3. 3) Process payroll using HRIS/payroll system.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in an HR process can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. 1) Collect and verify employee data (hours, overtime).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn HR work, the useful decision depends on the role, candidate or employee context, policy, evidence, and applicable employment requirements. The process should be documented and applied consistently rather than driven by ad-hoc judgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
