    shortDescription: "Focus on mutability, thread-safety, and performance.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`String` is immutable, meaning any operation creates a new object, making it inefficient for heavy string manipulation. `StringBuffer` is mutable and thread-safe (synchronized methods), while `StringBuilder` is mutable and not thread-safe, making it the fastest for single-threaded operations.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can describe the difference between `String`, `StringBuilder`, and `StringBuffer`. accurately and connect the explanation to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `String` is immutable, meaning any operation creates a new object, making it inefficient for heavy string manipulation.\n2. `StringBuffer` is mutable and thread-safe (synchronized methods), while `StringBuilder` is mutable and not thread-safe, making it the fastest for single-threaded operations.\n3. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `String` is immutable, meaning any operation creates a new object, making it inefficient for heavy string manipulation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. `String` is immutable, meaning any operation creates a new object, making it inefficient for heavy string manipulation.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
