    question: "What is the difference between `==` and `.equals()` in Java?",
    slug: "equals-vs-operator",
    shortDescription: "Clarify reference comparison vs content comparison.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`==` compares memory addresses (references) for objects, checking if they point to the same instance. `.equals()` is a method intended to compare the content or logical equality of two objects, and it can be overridden by classes like `String`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `==` and `.equals()` in java and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `==` compares memory addresses (references) for objects, checking if they point to the same instance.\n2. `.equals()` is a method intended to compare the content or logical equality of two objects, and it can be overridden by classes like `String`.\n3. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `==` compares memory addresses (references) for objects, checking if they point to the same instance.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `==` compares memory addresses (references) for objects, checking if they point to the same instance.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
