    slug: "interface-segregation",
    shortDescription: "Part of SOLID.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nISP states that clients should not be forced to depend on interfaces they do not use. It means larger interfaces should be split into smaller, more specific ones.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can describe the Interface Segregation Principle (ISP). accurately and connect the explanation to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. ISP states that clients should not be forced to depend on interfaces they do not use.\n2. It means larger interfaces should be split into smaller, more specific ones.\n3. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nA service can depend on an interface such as `PaymentGateway`, allowing a production adapter and a test double to implement the same contract.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. ISP states that clients should not be forced to depend on interfaces they do not use.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
