    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDIP states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions (interfaces). Abstractions should not depend on details; details should depend on abstractions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can describe the Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP). accurately and connect the explanation to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. DIP states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions (interfaces).\n2. Abstractions should not depend on details; details should depend on abstractions.\n3. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. DIP states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions (interfaces).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. DIP states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions (interfaces).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
