    slug: "checked-vs-unchecked",
    shortDescription: "Define the two types of exceptions.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nChecked exceptions (e.g., `IOException`, `SQLException`) are checked at compile-time. The compiler forces you to handle them (using try-catch or `throws`). Unchecked exceptions (e.g., `NullPointerException`, `ArithmeticException`) are subclasses of `RuntimeException` and are not checked at compile-time.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between checked and unchecked exceptions and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Checked exceptions (e.g., `IOException`, `SQLException`) are checked at compile-time.\n2. The compiler forces you to handle them (using try-catch or `throws`).\n3. Unchecked exceptions (e.g., `NullPointerException`, `ArithmeticException`) are subclasses of `RuntimeException` and are not checked at compile-time.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Checked exceptions (e.g., `IOException`, `SQLException`) are checked at compile-time.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Checked exceptions (e.g., `IOException`, `SQLException`) are checked at compile-time.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
