    question: "What is the difference between `wait()` and `sleep()`?",
    slug: "wait-vs-sleep",
    shortDescription: "Compare the methods.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`wait()` is an instance method of `Object` that releases the monitor/lock and causes the thread to wait until notified. It must be called within a synchronized block. `sleep()` is a static method of `Thread` that pauses execution for a specified time, does not release any locks, and does not require synchronization.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `wait()` and `sleep()` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `wait()` is an instance method of `Object` that releases the monitor/lock and causes the thread to wait until notified.\n2. It must be called within a synchronized block.\n3. `sleep()` is a static method of `Thread` that pauses execution for a specified time, does not release any locks, and does not require synchronization.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `wait()` is an instance method of `Object` that releases the monitor/lock and causes the thread to wait until notified.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `wait()` is an instance method of `Object` that releases the monitor/lock and causes the thread to wait until notified.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
