    shortDescription: "Differentiate the test types.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUnit testing focuses on testing individual components/classes in isolation, mocking dependencies. Integration testing focuses on verifying the interaction between components (e.g., database, external services, or modules) to ensure they work correctly together.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between unit testing and integration testing and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Unit testing focuses on testing individual components/classes in isolation, mocking dependencies.\n2. Integration testing focuses on verifying the interaction between components (e.g., database, external services, or modules) to ensure they work correctly together.\n3. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Unit testing focuses on testing individual components/classes in isolation, mocking dependencies.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Unit testing focuses on testing individual components/classes in isolation, mocking dependencies.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
