    slug: "virtual-thread-vs-platform",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two thread types.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPlatform threads are thin wrappers around OS threads, expensive to create. Virtual threads are user-mode threads managed by the JVM, decoupled from OS threads. They are extremely lightweight, allowing millions of virtual threads to coexist, dramatically simplifying high-concurrency applications.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is a `virtual thread` vs a `platform thread` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Platform threads are thin wrappers around OS threads, expensive to create.\n2. Virtual threads are user-mode threads managed by the JVM, decoupled from OS threads.\n3. They are extremely lightweight, allowing millions of virtual threads to coexist, dramatically simplifying high-concurrency applications.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nA request-heavy Java service can run many blocking I/O tasks on virtual threads, while still limiting CPU-bound work according to available cores and downstream capacity.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Platform threads are thin wrappers around OS threads, expensive to create.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
