    slug: "lazy-vs-eager-performance",
    shortDescription: "Discuss performance trade-offs.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`EAGER` can cause performance issues because it loads unnecessary data, especially in large object graphs. `LAZY` avoids loading data until needed, but can lead to `LazyInitializationException` if accessed outside the persistence context.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `fetchtype.lazy` and `fetchtype.eager` performance implications and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `EAGER` can cause performance issues because it loads unnecessary data, especially in large object graphs.\n2. `LAZY` avoids loading data until needed, but can lead to `LazyInitializationException` if accessed outside the persistence context.\n3. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a Java backend service, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `EAGER` can cause performance issues because it loads unnecessary data, especially in large object graphs.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a Java backend service, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `EAGER` can cause performance issues because it loads unnecessary data, especially in large object graphs.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
