    slug: "js-vs-ecmascript",
    shortDescription: "Compare JavaScript and ECMAScript.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nECMAScript is the specification that defines the core language. JavaScript is the implementation of that specification (most commonly in browsers and Node.js). ECMAScript versions (ES6, ES2015, etc.) define new language features that JavaScript engines adopt.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between javascript and ecmascript and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. ECMAScript is the specification that defines the core language.\n2. JavaScript is the implementation of that specification (most commonly in browsers and Node.js).\n3. ECMAScript versions (ES6, ES2015, etc.) define new language features that JavaScript engines adopt.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. ECMAScript is the specification that defines the core language.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. ECMAScript is the specification that defines the core language.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
