    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nType coercion is the automatic conversion of values from one data type to another (e.g., `'5' - 2` converts string to number). Coercion happens with operators like `==`, `+`, `-`, etc. Use `===` to avoid unexpected coercion.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain type coercion in JavaScript accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Type coercion is the automatic conversion of values from one data type to another (e.g., `'5' - 2` converts string to number).\n2. Coercion happens with operators like `==`, `+`, `-`, etc.\n3. Use `===` to avoid unexpected coercion.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Type coercion is the automatic conversion of values from one data type to another (e.g., `'5' - 2` converts string to number).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Type coercion is the automatic conversion of values from one data type to another (e.g., `'5' - 2` converts string to number).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
