    slug: "function-declaration-vs-expression",
    shortDescription: "Compare declaration and expression.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFunction declarations are hoisted and can be called before definition. Function expressions are not hoisted; they are created when the assignment is reached. Example: `function foo() {}` vs `const foo = function() {}`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between function declaration and function expression and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Function declarations are hoisted and can be called before definition.\n2. Function expressions are not hoisted; they are created when the assignment is reached.\n3. Example: `function foo() {}` vs `const foo = function() {}`.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Function declarations are hoisted and can be called before definition.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Function declarations are hoisted and can be called before definition.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
