    question: "What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous code?",
    slug: "sync-vs-async-js",
    shortDescription: "Compare synchronous and asynchronous.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSynchronous code executes sequentially, blocking further execution until complete. Asynchronous code allows other tasks to run while waiting for I/O (like network requests), using callbacks, promises, or async/await.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous code and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Synchronous code executes sequentially, blocking further execution until complete.\n2. Asynchronous code allows other tasks to run while waiting for I/O (like network requests), using callbacks, promises, or async/await.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nAn `async` function can await a fetch promise; the current function yields while the I/O is pending and continues when the promise settles.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Synchronous code executes sequentially, blocking further execution until complete.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
