    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCallbacks are passed as arguments; promises use chaining. Promises provide better error handling (`.catch`), avoid callback hell, and support composition (`Promise.all`, `Promise.race`).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a callback and a promise and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Callbacks are passed as arguments; promises use chaining.\n2. Promises provide better error handling (`.catch`), avoid callback hell, and support composition (`Promise.all`, `Promise.race`).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nAn `async` function can await a fetch promise; the current function yields while the I/O is pending and continues when the promise settles.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Callbacks are passed as arguments; promises use chaining.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
